title: PoV size reclaim fix
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |
    This PR introduces incremental storage root estimation for PoV reclaim, addressing
    issue #6020. Storage weight reclaim allows parachains to return unused PoV space
    from completed extrinsics, but previously didn't account for storage root calculation.

    When items are written or deleted during extrinsic execution, they're only noted in
    the overlay. However, when calculating the storage root at block finalization, trie
    nodes must be accessed to insert/delete/merge the changes. These trie node accesses
    are recorded in the proof, causing the actual PoV size to exceed estimates.

    The solution introduces incremental storage root estimation that approximates trie
    node accesses that would occur during storage root calculation, without actually
    computing the root hash. This is triggered after each extrinsic via the weight reclaim
    pallet using a new version 2 of the storage_proof_size host function.

    The estimation also needs a state-machine fix for runtimes that compute a storage root
    in the middle of a block. It changes what such runtimes observe, so it comes as a new
    host function version and the existing version keeps its exact behaviour:
    - storage::append version 2 drops a storage root cached by an earlier storage::root.
      Version 1 keeps it, so the block's root and storage transaction can miss the append.

    The bodies of the old and the new version are separate, callable items:
    Externalities::storage_append and storage_append_v2.

    storage::append version 2 and storage_proof_size version 2 are registered only: nodes
    provide them, but runtimes keep calling the old versions, and the validate_block shims
    in cumulus-pallet-parachain-system keep answering the old versions. The two markers
    must be dropped together, in the same release that switches the two shims
    (host_storage_append, host_storage_proof_size) to the new versions.

    A child trie root computed in the middle of a block no longer writes that root into the
    main storage overlay. Proofs of blocks that compute child roots mid-block therefore contain
    fewer nodes, so the collator node and the runtime must run matching state-machine code,
    which the estimation already requires.
